{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791556245,"nonce":"yu+I6IsH8I/IhsSlVA/NnsuP7zs=","title":"Reflect.callMethod reads arguments as raw slot bits, so Int/Float mix and null-for-default fail (Issue3826 testReflect)","message":"function get(a = 2, b = 4.25) return \"\"+a+\"/\"+b;\n    function two(a:Int, b:Float) ...\n    Reflect.callMethod(this, two, [5, 8.5])    // \"0/8.5\", Haxe \"5/8.5\"\n    Reflect.callMethod(this, get, [null, 8.5]) // \"0/8.5\", Haxe \"2/8.5\"\n    Reflect.callMethod(this, get, [null])      // \"0/4.25\", Haxe \"2/4.25\"\n    Reflect.callMethod(this, get, [dval,dval]) // \"6/3e-323\", Haxe \"6/6\"  (dval:Dynamic = 6)\n\n`lower_reflect_call_method` (ir/mir/expr/reflect.rs) reads each element with `call_method_slot` (haxe_array_get_i64 + haxe_unbox_erased_return) and then bitcasts or truncates the raw bits to the parameter's IR type. `[5, 8.5]` is an Array\u003cFloat\u003e, so 5 arrives as f64 bits and an Int parameter reads 0; a boxed Int for a Float parameter is reinterpreted as float bits. `call_method_arg` applies a default only when the array is SHORTER, never for a null element, and `defaults` is empty when `get` is a bound method value (callee not resolved to a function id).\n\nFix direction: read each element as a box by the array's element type, convert box -\u003e parameter type with Haxe's coercions (Int\u003c-\u003eFloat), and substitute a literal default for null. Same reading as the direct-call rule in default_for_null_arg (signatures.rs). Probe: nuc ~/probes/prefl. Rows: Issue3826 (last remaining failure after the direct-call fix).","files":null}]}